module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.heal_round_one_bootstraps_from_the_mirror_the_change_must_repair

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

data heal_round_one_bootstraps_from_the_mirror_the_change_must_repair: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "heal_round_one_bootstraps_from_the_mirror_the_change_must_repair" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed, blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt, trigger_capability: "heal round 1 always bootstrapping from the BASE ref's committed stage0 mirror, never the pull request branch's own, with a receipt naming the mirror it built from; selected unconditionally, not as a fallback after a branch-mirror build refuses (DESIGN section 5: a fallback would be a second path). Sufficient for: no refusal that a pull request's own .dag repairs can block that pull request's regeneration." } },

  receipts: [
    "**heal round 1 bootstraps from the mirror the change must repair** (a bootstrap deadlock). Heal's first round builds the seed from the pull request branch's OWN committed stage0 mirror. When that mirror carries a refusal that the same pull request's .dag fixes, the seed that heal builds refuses the very source whose regeneration would remove the refusal, so the change cannot regenerate itself.",

    "INVALID STATE: a regeneration round whose builder is a projection of the state under repair. The mirror on the branch is an output of an earlier round; using it as the input to the next round makes the fix a precondition of its own installation.",

    "WHY IT IS NOT A FAILING CHANGE: the .dag is correct and v2-bootstrap would accept it from a correct mirror. The refusal reads as the change's own defect, so the cheap response is to weaken or work around the .dag, which is the DESIGN section 5 concealment rather than a repair.",

    "SPECIMEN (gunb-ai/gunbc#13936, head 4377851b539a). Heal (run 40064940799, job heal-generated-artifacts) and v2-bootstrap (run 40064940687) both refused with EmittedCompilerBuildFailed, shared layer refused for 'Nat': TypeDeclNotDeclared, at the emitted accessors of std.measure PositiveMeasureCount and credits_per_minute and of std.checked_arithmetic PrefixedLineNat and prefixed_line_last_space_nat (the four declarations in evidence; those runs print the mirror positions). The installed mirror came from auto-heal commit 44903b32b6d9, which installed the shared-type re-key WITHOUT the accompanying fix, so the branch's own stage0 built a seed that refused the branch's .dag.",

    "REMEDY USED: reset the branch's stage0 mirror to the base ref's and let heal regenerate. That is the manual form of the trigger capability, applied by hand to one pull request.",

    "SPECIMEN 2 (gunb-ai/gunbc#15175, head 432809a3ed4b). The round was refused E0599 — a variant the pull request's own seed edit newly declares was constructed by code at the head while the generated side of that call was base's. The cause was the SHAPE of the restore, not its intent: round 1 restored only the TOP-LEVEL generated mirror names, so a NESTED partition kept the head's bytes while every top-level file held base's, and the crate the seed compiled was neither base nor head. A base/head chimera, refused in the seed build of the host that must emit the repair.",

    "SPECIMEN 3 (gunb-ai/gunbc#14876, heal run 40739785316). Twenty-six rows of E0308 and E0282 in `v1-stage0-std-core` at the same `Build the regenerator` step — the same shape one level deeper: base's types meeting the head's expressions inside the crate the seed build compiles, because the files the restore skipped were the ones the pull request had edited.",

    "SPECIMEN 4 (the after-effect of the fix this row's trigger capability describes; gunb-ai/gunbc#14876, heal run 41061363628, job 124081920078; gunb-ai/gunbc#15310, heal run 41098380134; gunb-ai/gunbc#15441, heal run 41121984158; gunb-ai/gunbc#15455 at a598c85, heal run 41154136317). Whole-crate round 1 writes base's bytes over every path in the base commit's own tree, and the teardown renames the journaled head bytes back — over 306 paths whose bytes were never different, because those pull requests touch no stage0 file at all. Rewriting identical bytes bumps a file's mtime, and cargo fingerprints a path dependency by mtime, so the measured partition rebuild recompiles crates the round's OWN derived scope had excluded, and the scope guard then refuses a round that had done nothing wrong: StageSeedBuildRefused, compiled excluded packages [v1-stage0-runtime], with changed_mirrors=[std_algebra.rs, v1_compiler_infer_method.rs, v1_compiler_infer_resolve.rs, v1_compiler_infer_types.rs], owning_packages=[v1-stage0-std-core, v1-stage0-v1-infer] and package_closure=[std-core, v1-infer, std-surface, extdeps-languages, v1-artifact, emit-core, v1-compiler]; on #15310, six excluded partition packages with changed_mirrors=[v1_compiler_emit_rust.rs] and closure=[v1-compiler]; on #15441, excluded [v1-stage0-runtime, std-core, std-surface, extdeps-languages, v1-artifact]; on #15455, compiled excluded packages [v1-stage0-runtime] with changed_mirrors=[v1_compiler_compile.rs, v1_compiler_compiler_tests_rust.rs, v1_compiler_ownership.rs, v1_std_core.rs]. The mechanism is INFERRED, not observed: no cargo fingerprint line was ever captured, because seed_cargo_build parsed only cargo's Compiling lines and discarded the rest — which the same change now fixes.",

    "WHY THE GUARD WAS RIGHT AND THE WRITER WAS WRONG: the derived closure is computed from the mirrors the round actually changed, and v1-stage0-runtime reads none of them — the only thing that made cargo compile it was a fresh mtime on files whose content never moved. The repair is therefore at the writer, per DESIGN section 2 (no redundant work): a write whose bytes already equal the target is skipped in BOTH the round-1 seed and the journal restore, and only the paths the seed moved are journalled. The scope guard is NOT widened, and mtimes are NOT restored — a restored mtime is a second lie on the same file. The control is a_stage0_untouched_pr_moves_no_mtime_in_the_seed_or_the_restore, whose second arm is a discriminator so the mtime assertion cannot pass vacuously.",

    "THE SHAPE THAT MADE BOTH SPECIMENS POSSIBLE: the restore population was a hand-kept NAME LIST (top-level generated mirror basenames), so membership was a property of the list rather than of the base commit. Anything the list did not name — a nested partition, a hand-maintained file, the crate manifest, the build script — silently kept the head's bytes. A population read from the base commit's own tree has no such list to fall out of date.",

    "RUNG: mitigatable. The deadlock is loud (a typed refusal, not silent wrongness), and an operator who recognises it can discharge it by the manual reset; nothing prevents the next pull request reaching the same state.",

    "FIX LANDED: round 1 takes its population from the BASE COMMIT'S OWN TREE — every path under the crate the seed build compiles (`git ls-tree -r --name-only <base commit> -- src/v1/stage0`), with NO filter of any kind: nested partitions, hand-maintained files inside the crate, the crate manifest and the build script alike. A path base never committed is kept as the pull request wrote it (that is its authored material), and a path the base tree lacks is counted where the reader sees it. There is no fallback: if the base commit cannot be named, or a base blob cannot be read, or the rollback journal cannot be written, the round REFUSES before moving a byte. The receipt names the source — `base_mirror_commit=<sha> crate=src/v1/stage0 crate_paths=<n> head_added_paths_kept=<k>` — so a round that built from anything but that commit is visible in the round's own output. BOTH images of a relinking round read ONE value: the image that seeds takes it from what its own seed did, and the re-exec'd image that reports — which seeds nothing under the handoff rule — DERIVES it from the same read-only authority, so the relinked run cannot fall to the head arm; an unresolvable base commit refuses with `BootstrapBaseMirrorCommitUnresolved` rather than dropping the line.",

    "CONTROL ON THE FIX: `v1-stage0` `required_regen_host` tests `the_whole_crate_authority_repairs_the_nested_and_hand_directory_chimera` (a nested mirror and a hand-maintained file whose head bytes are the damage hold base bytes after the bootstrap, and the rollback returns the pull request's own bytes for both), `hand_maintained_files_and_directories_survive_the_bootstrap` (the base population is exactly the crate's committed paths, and the authored `.dag` outside it is untouched), and `a_refusal_before_the_bootstrap_moves_no_bytes` (an unwritable journal refuses with `BootstrapBaseMirrorJournal` and leaves every byte where it was). The first is red on the pre-fix population, which restored only top-level generated names. HONEST LIMIT: these are `#[cfg(test)]` tests and no required CI step runs the v1 unit suite, so they establish the fix by execution when run, not on every pull — which is why this row stays at mitigatable rather than claiming mechanically preventable. The next-rung trigger for THAT half is the same enrolment the seed-build row names: a required step that runs the unit suite.",

    "CEILING: structurally guaranteed. Which mirror round 1 builds from is a decidable policy fact of heal. If round 1 always builds from the base ref's mirror, a branch mirror can never be the builder of its own repair, and the state has no route into an accepted heal round.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, per stern-bat-401's ruling: heal round 1 ALWAYS bootstraps from the base ref's mirror and emits a receipt naming the mirror used. It is NOT a fallback taken when the branch-mirror build fails: a fallback is a second path beside the modeled route (DESIGN section 5) and would hide which mirror produced a given round. That heal change is routed separately; this row names it and builds nothing.",

    "REVIEW TELL: a heal or v2-bootstrap refusal on a pull request whose cited sites sit in generated mirror files, and whose .dag change is the repair of exactly those sites. Ask which mirror built the seed before diagnosing the change.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.measure", decl_name: "PositiveMeasureCount", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.measure", decl_name: "credits_per_minute", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.prefixed_line", decl_name: "PrefixedLineNat", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.prefixed_line", decl_name: "prefixed_line_last_space_nat", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
